WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.590
- This feels like an obvious thing to say, but:

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:04.200
tanks are expensive,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.730
both to buy and to fuel.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.320
If you want to train someone to drive one,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.940
it'd be great if they
could learn the basics

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.380
on a simulator first.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.510
So in the 1970s, the Swiss Army
had the idea

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.210
to design and build tank simulators.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.310
But this was well before
computer-generated images.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.880
They couldn't create a virtual world.
So instead...

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.840
...they built a wonderful example
of 70s analog technology.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.140
The viewscreen in that box
is connected to a real camera

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.930
and a real miniature
landscape through there.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.810
In the past, thousands and thousands
of tank drivers

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.670
trained on machines like that.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.830
There's now only one
simulator left in the world

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:41.190
and it's in the Swiss Military Museum,
about an hour north of Zurich.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.350
And tourists can try it.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.540
- There existed in the past,

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.590
12 simulators for
different types of tanks.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:53.820
It's very accurate for
teaching the basics,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.630
and teaching where to go with
the hand and with the feet.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.190
- Put that over your head.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:03.150
And then because in a normal tank
it's too loud with the motor noises,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.800
you have a special
microphone that goes around,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.240
- Throat mic!
- It's a throat mic.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.850
- So this goes on like...

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.860
- This.
- Oh, that is...

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.150
Okay, that's interesting.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.650
Yep. Okay.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:14.650
- Headset.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:17.820
- Okay.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.530
- Looks good.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.363
- Let's drive a tank.

00:01:20.363 --> 00:01:22.072
- Yeah, I wish you a good drive.

00:01:22.072 --> 00:01:22.923
- Thank you!

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.460
Wow. Okay.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.801
All right, so.

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:29.220
- So, I'm going to start the hydraulics.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.240
- Yep.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.960
Oh boy! Okay. [laughs]

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.340
- Sounds good.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.544
Now, you can start the main motor.

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:41.850
[motor revving]

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.020
- Very well. Sounds good.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:52.925
- You can drive in the middle.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.103
- It's a tank.

00:01:55.936 --> 00:01:57.360
- Yeah, sure.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.006
You can go in the fourth gear.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.133
- Yeah.

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:10.050
- That's the only one of the
Type 68 tank that's remaining

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.910
and we managed to get it running here.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.060
It took roughly about
one and a half, two years.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.030
We had to really go into it
from ground-up.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.730
It was very difficult.
Not for everything,

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.310
but mostly for the electrical
and electronic parts

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.170
because it's old tech.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.320
It's not like you can go to
any supermarket and buy it.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.059
- Fifth gear.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.398
- There is a memory function
on the computer module

00:02:51.398 --> 00:02:57.715
which is based off this x and y axis,
says you're here, you're on gravel,

00:02:57.715 --> 00:03:02.490
you're on snow, you're on normal earth,
you're on road, and something like that.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.390
If you drive into a tree, the feet
where the camera sits on

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.855
just moves up and it's like
you're having a panoramic ride

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:12.390
above our model with a helicopter!

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.223
- Okay, we can go...

00:03:17.895 --> 00:03:18.728
- Fifth gear, fifth gear.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.621
- The foot where you're driving on,
it's kind of a metal slide.

00:03:26.621 --> 00:03:29.220
If that moves a tiny bit.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.770
Or for instance, if there's
some contact with it

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:32.760
and it moves, for instance,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.817
it needs to go up to go over that,

00:03:34.817 --> 00:03:37.620
it will notice that
and signalize the cabin

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.630
that you're moving
over something.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.900
- The camera's original.
The foot is original.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.670
The whole model is original.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.940
Everything except for the
computer part is original,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.150
but it was too old and you don't
find parts easily for that.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.490
So we had to rebuild it
on a Raspberry Pi.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.890
- You can go into fourth gear
again if you're comfortable.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.720
- It is really dangerous what you do.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.633
Here, it's a game.
It's like a game.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.813
This job is not a game.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.680
- Thank you very much.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.150
- You're welcome.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.817
- [laughing] How do I get out of here?

